; `effect(div)` is a claim about something the compiler never looks
; for, so it is AX3037 - unverifiable - and not AX3010.
;
; This was a trap the day AX3010 became an error. `Div` is a built-in
; effect name that any claim can spell and the walk has NO PRODUCER
; for: nothing anywhere contributes it. So `missing Div` was never a
; fact about the body - it is a fact about the analysis - and once
; AX3010 refused builds, a `div` claim went from a warning nobody had
; to act on to a build that cannot be fixed except by deleting the
; claim. A diagnostic no edit can satisfy is worse than none.
;
; Inferring it is not the answer, and that is measured rather than
; assumed: the cheapest sound rule - a self-call, or any `while` -
; marks 65% of this compiler divergent and calls the syntax zoo's own
; counted loop non-terminating.
;
; `diverges` really does not return, and still draws the warning,
; which is the point: the walk does not infer divergence for a body
; that HAS it either. The claim is recorded and nothing validates it.
;
; `Err` is NOT in the same position, which is worth stating because
; the effect table reads as though it were. `Err` is spellable in a
; HANDLE list, but the AXTAG claim path lowercases its value and finds
; no declaration, so `;@axiom:effect(err)` is read as an undeclared
; CUSTOM effect and reports `missing err`. That is already the right
; answer - you named an effect that does not exist - and it needs no
; special case. `div` is the only value that resolves to a built-in
; nothing produces.
;
; `honest` is the control. A claim naming an effect that HAS a
; producer stays AX3010 when the body does not support it - the split
; is per-effect, not a blanket softening of the check.
(:: quiet (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(div)
(fn (quiet n) n)

(:: diverges (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(div)
(fn (diverges n) (diverges n))

(:: honest (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(mut)
(fn (honest n) n)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (quiet 1))
